WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.690
An impact transfers kinetic energy into target rock, causing shock, fragmentation

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:10.390
and sometimes melting. Excavated fragments are thrown outward and form ejecta

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:13.470
around a crater. Smaller events leave simple craters;

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.710
larger ones can produce complex craters with central peaks.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:21.150
Shape reflects formation conditions, not a universal rule that one crater

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:21.990
type is older.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:26.330
Impacts were much more frequent when solar-system debris was abundant.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.790
Earth’s interior also retained formation heat and more radioactive heat production.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:36.310
Early volcanism included very hot ultramafic lava, and slightly metamorphosed lava

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:40.310
survives as greenstone. The textbook treats the onset and style of

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.990
early plate tectonics cautiously; present processes need not have operated at

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:45.710
today’s rates.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.260
Light gases from early accretion were readily lost.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.900
Volcanic outgassing supplied water vapor, carbon dioxide and other gases, while

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.960
water eventually collected into oceans.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.340
The early atmosphere lacked significant free oxygen.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.440
Do not confuse oxygen bound inside minerals with oxygen gas available

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.540
in air or dissolved in seawater.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.280
Cyanobacteria released oxygen through photosynthesis.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.220
Dissolved iron could then oxidize and precipitate, helping form banded iron-rich

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.440
deposits composed mainly of iron oxides and quartz.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:23.940
Oxygen production and atmospheric oxygen accumulation are separate steps because oxygen

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.880
also reacts with surrounding materials.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.220
The rock record preserves evidence of these chemical changes.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.467
The supplied textbook places the Great Oxygenation Event around two point

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.760
three to two point four billion years ago.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.040
Oxygen stayed low through much of the Proterozoic, then rose again

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.540
before the Cambrian. The cause and timing of the second transition

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.800
remain research questions in the source.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.760
Relate the interpreted curve to the rocks supporting it.
